<p class="kicker">Philosophy · How it fits together</p>

# Product

<p class="lead">One design system, several apps. <strong>Llama UI</strong> draws the interface and handles interaction; each app owns its state and talks to llama-server.</p>

## The Llama stack

- **llama.cpp** runs models locally; **llama-server** is its HTTP server.
- **Llama UI** is the component library this Storybook documents.
- **Llama Chat** is the chat client built from it. It replaces the legacy web UI and ships inside llama-server.
- **Llama for Mac** and **Llama for Windows** run llama-server from the menu bar or the system tray and open Llama Chat in their own window.
- **llama.brand** is the one source of the icon, the fonts and the colours.

<Figure n={1} caption="The products and their repositories. llama.brand supplies the brand; llama.cpp holds the engine and Llama UI; the native apps run the engine and open Llama Chat.">
	<Products />
</Figure>

## One library, many apps

Llama UI holds UI and UX only, no business logic. Llama Chat is the first app built on it, and the llama.app website uses it too, so models look and behave the same in both. Keeping the library separate lets anyone build their own app on it.

## Llama UI draws, the apps decide

Components take data in and report what the user did. State and calls to the server belong to the app.

<Figure n={2} caption="What belongs in Llama UI and what belongs in an app. A chat app is the library's parts plus the app's own logic.">
	<Rule />
</Figure>

## Across the three layers

- **Model:** a GGUF file from Hugging Face, which the apps help find, download and keep.
- **Service:** llama-server, run by hand, started by a native app, or shared by someone else.
- **Application:** Llama UI and the apps built on it. They never assume a cloud account and always show which model runs, and where.

## Simple first, depth on request

The first run offers one suggested model, small and multimodal. The Llama app downloads it, configures it and runs llama-server, with no terminal. Llama Chat opens on a conversation, not on settings. Every other model and server setting sits behind a disclosure, with a short explanation where a term needs one.

## One build, three channels

Llama Chat is a static app (HTML, JavaScript and CSS), built once per release. Today it ships inside llama-server. Planned: `npx llama-chat` and chat.llama.app, which carry the client only and connect to a server you add.

<Figure n={3} caption="The same build in three channels. Only the bundled one comes with a server.">
	<Channels />
</Figure>

The app will tell where it runs at runtime, with no build flags: if its own origin answers as llama.cpp, it runs bundled; otherwise it asks for a server.

<p class="colophon">After the Llama Suite planning pages: Llama UI Stack, and One build, three channels.</p>
